{"name":"Peach Crème Brûlée","hex":"#ffe19d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"peach","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any tree of species Prunus persica, native to China and now widely cultivated throughout temperate regions, having pink flowers and edible fruit.","Soft juicy stone fruit of the peach tree, having yellow flesh, downy, red-tinted yellow skin, and a deeply sculptured pit or stone containing a single seed."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the color peach.","Particularly pleasing or agreeable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peach"},{"term":"crème","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Synonym of cream."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very sugary, fluffy white cream derivative or creamlike analogue.","Cream."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cr%C3%A8me"},{"term":"brûlée","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of crème brûlée."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To create a sugary crust (on food), using a chef's torch."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crème brûlée","blocks":[{"text":"Crème brûlée, also known as burnt cream, Cambridge burnt cream, or Trinity cream, and virtually identical to crema catalana, is a dessert consisting of a rich custard base topped with a layer of hardened caramelized sugar. It is normally served slightly chilled; the heat from the caramelizing process tends to warm the top of the custard, while leaving the center cool. The custard base is generally flavored with vanilla in French cuisine, but can have other flavorings. It is sometimes garnished with fruit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/2014_0531_Cr%C3%A8me_br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e_Doi_Mae_Salong_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-2014_0531_Cr%C3%A8me_br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e_Doi_Mae_Salong_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cr%C3%A8me_br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e","wikidata":"Q312839"},{"title":"Peach","blocks":[{"text":"The peach is a deciduous tree that bears edible juicy fruits with various characteristics. Most are simply called peaches, while the glossy-skinned, non-fuzzy varieties are called nectarines. Though from the same species, they are regarded commercially as different fruits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg/330px-Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach","wikidata":"Q13189"},{"title":"Peach (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Peach is a color that is named for the pale color of the interior flesh of the peach fruit. Like the color apricot, the color peach is paler than most actual peach fruits and seems to have been formulated primarily to create a pastel palette of colors for interior design."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach_(color)","wikidata":"Q5148721"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Cr%C3%A8me_br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e_%284554822506%29.jpg/500px-Cr%C3%A8me_br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e_%284554822506%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Cr%C3%A8me_br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e_%284554822506%29.jpg/1280px-Cr%C3%A8me_br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e_%284554822506%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Cr%C3%A8me_br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e_%284554822506%29.jpg/500px-Cr%C3%A8me_br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e_%284554822506%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The sugar being caramelized with a blowtorch","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACr%C3%A8me_br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e_(4554822506).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikime